WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.850
A student team promised a field sensor

2
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.890
would run for a year on two AA cells.

3
00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:08.750
It died after nine days.

4
00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.650
The radio was never switched off.

5
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.560
Today: how to budget energy with duty

6
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.380
cycles, what real-time really means, and

7
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.880
how to schedule tasks so deadlines are

8
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:22.100
met.

9
00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.390
Battery life is capacity

10
00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:27.390
divided by average current.

11
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.800
A device that sleeps most of the

12
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.240
time has an average set by how

13
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.060
long and how often it wakes.

14
00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.090
That fraction is the duty cycle.

15
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.800
Awake at twenty milliamps for one

16
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.880
second in sixty, and asleep at ten

17
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.900
microamps otherwise, the average is

18
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.500
about a third of a milliamp.

19
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.340
Two thousand milliamp hours then

20
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.040
last roughly eight months.

21
00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:57.330
In a typical LoRa sensor node, the radio

22
00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.210
uses most of the energy, even though it is

23
00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.290
on for a tiny fraction of the time.

24
00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.100
Sensing and the processor use far less.

25
00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:09.910
Real-time does not mean fast.

26
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.740
It means every task finishes

27
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.020
before its deadline.

28
00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.800
A hard real-time miss is a

29
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.420
failure, like an airbag.

30
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.400
A soft miss only lowers quality,

31
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.220
like a dropped video frame.

32
00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:25.890
Give the shortest period

33
00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:27.410
the highest priority.

34
00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.850
Liu and Layland proved that n periodic

35
00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.510
tasks always meet their deadlines this way

36
00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:37.410
if total utilisation is below n times two

37
00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.370
to the one over n, minus one.

38
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.330
For many tasks that tends to

39
00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.110
about sixty nine percent.

40
00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:45.890
Quick check!

41
00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:49.090
A node draws fifty milliamps for

42
00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:51.210
one second every hundred seconds

43
00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:52.810
and almost nothing otherwise.

44
00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.620
What is its average current?

45
00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.870
Half a milliamp: fifty

46
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:00.430
times one in a hundred.

47
00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.450
So halving the wake time doubles

48
00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.730
battery life far more cheaply

49
00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:06.730
than a bigger battery.

50
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.060
In the app, open the battery lab.

51
00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.320
Change how often the device wakes and

52
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.280
how long the radio stays on, and watch

53
00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.680
the predicted battery life.

54
00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:19.470
Your turn.

55
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.700
Two tasks: one needs one

56
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.140
millisecond every four, the other

57
00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.220
needs two milliseconds every ten.

58
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.240
Is the total utilisation under the

59
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.940
rate-monotonic bound for two tasks,

60
00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:34.180
about eighty three percent?

61
00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.160
Yes: a quarter plus a fifth

62
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.680
is forty five percent.

63
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.140
Remember: battery life follows average

64
00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.060
current, the radio dominates, and

65
00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.260
real-time means meeting deadlines.

66
00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.670
Try it in your next lab: give each team a

67
00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.790
current meter and their own sensor node.

68
00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.200
They measure active and sleep

69
00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.240
current, then predict battery

70
00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:00.860
life and defend the number.
